AN ACTION CANNOT REACH ITS ARGUMENT'S PARENT. EVERY OTHER DIRECTION EXISTS.

WHAT IS BROKEN. incant/utilities' displayIfVisible opens with

    :parent x y width height;

which is meant to hoist the CONTAINER's box so the argument's own box can be
tested against it. There is no way to write that. `parent` is not an accessor,
so the bare name resolves to an empty local, aCTionScopeXP finds nothing in it
(`lookin[name]` misses), calls grup.clear() on each of x/y/width/height, and
the locals are left carrying NO DATA. Under bear-trap #26 a no-data field
returns its own TAG from .text, so the arithmetic two lines later says

    ERROR Operator + failed on py and ph
    ERROR Operator + failed on px and pw

and the message names an operator while the defect is a missing accessor.

THE GAP IS ONE DIRECTION OUT OF SIX. opDot (Instruct.rtn:340-351) implements
nextInParent (401), priorInParent (402), firstInList (403), lastInList (404)
and nextMember (405). Sideways and downward are covered. UP IS NOT.

WHAT WAS MEASURED, one run, with a control that reads correctly so the misses
cannot be dismissed as a broken probe:

    control  argument.taG   -> fpInside     correct
    row a    .parent        -> fpA          the local's own tag echoed
    row b    .parenT        -> argument     bound to the argument, not its parent
    row c    .PARENT        -> fpC          tag echo

Three spellings, three different wrong answers, no error from any of them.

WHAT WAS ALREADY FIXED AND IS NOT THIS ISSUE. displayIfVisible carried four
defects; three were unambiguous and are mended:
    1  four semicolon-less `if`s          (the SemI- ruling)
    2  `:x y width height;`               lookin was `x`; now `:argument ...`
    3  `print ... argument.tag`           tok spelling; now .taG, prints dvInside
Only the parent road is left, and it is the one that needs a ruling.

NEXT: BEST GUESS

TWO SHAPES. Explicitly NOT a recommendation of one over the other by fiat --
the second is cheaper and the first is what the tree seems to want.

  A  ADD A PARENT ACCESSOR to opDot -- one more case beside 401-405, plus a
     decoder line and a fixture. GRADE: strong. It closes a gap that is
     conspicuous rather than arguable (five navigation cases exist and only
     "up" is missing), it makes the EXISTING source correct instead of asking
     every caller to compensate, and Tony's stated reason for keeping this
     function -- forms processing -- walks trees constantly and will want
     "up" everywhere, not once.
     WHAT WOULD PROMOTE IT: Tony ruling the accessor set is his to extend.
     WHAT WOULD KILL IT: a reason parent is deliberately not exposed --
     e.g. it would let incant code walk out of a scope it should not leave.
     That reason is not known to me and I did not find one in the tree.

  B  CHANGE THE CONTRACT so the container arrives explicitly -- as an
     attribute on the argument, per the multi-arg idiom (attributes plus a
     :scope statement). GRADE: workable, no language change, lands today.
     WHAT WOULD KILL IT: it pushes wiring to every caller and leaves the
     "up" gap in place for the next tree-walking action to rediscover.

WHY IT IS NOT DECIDED HERE. A is a language addition and this SEQ's scope was
a repair. Naming the shape is the deliverable; choosing it is Tony's.

A THIRD THING, CAPTURED SEPARATELY AND NOT PART OF THIS ISSUE. Even with the
parent road fixed, displayIfVisible's `if goodToGo;` tests a field that HOLDS
ZERO, and incant/andProbe already ruled that a bare field test goes by
PRESENCE, not value -- so it reads TRUE either way. andProbe's own note says
"the only shipping consumer of this shape is displayIfVisible in incant
utilities, which is defined and never called, so nothing live moved. The next
caller of that idiom is the one who gets bitten." That caller was this SEQ.
The function will need `if goodToGo == 1;` or the word form, whichever the
ruling on A produces.
